WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.287
Navigation interference threatens more than the map display.

00:00:03.287 --> 00:00:07.668
It can affect position hold, automated routes, return-to-home, geofencing, camera targeting,

00:00:07.668 --> 00:00:09.660
mapping overlap, flight logs, timestamps,

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:13.643
and the crew's shared understanding of where the aircraft is.

00:00:13.643 --> 00:00:16.431
Automated flight can continue using incorrect assumptions.

00:00:16.431 --> 00:00:19.618
A waypoint route may diverge, pause, or terminate.

00:00:19.618 --> 00:00:24.398
The pilot may spend critical time interpreting warnings while battery, distance, wind,

00:00:24.398 --> 00:00:26.390
and obstacles continue to change.

00:00:26.390 --> 00:00:27.983
Return-to-home deserves special caution.

00:00:27.983 --> 00:00:33.559
It depends on a correct home point and trustworthy current position, along with navigation,

00:00:33.559 --> 00:00:35.949
return altitude, wind, and obstacle assumptions.

00:00:35.949 --> 00:00:41.526
If those inputs are doubtful, automatic return may create risk rather than solve it.

00:00:41.526 --> 00:00:46.704
Geotagging and map products can become unreliable even when visible imagery remains useful.

00:00:46.704 --> 00:00:51.484
Analysts should not treat location metadata as authoritative after a navigation anomaly.

00:00:51.484 --> 00:00:54.272
Flag affected data for review and corroboration.

00:00:54.272 --> 00:00:57.061
Downstream systems may ingest those records automatically.

00:00:57.061 --> 00:00:58.654
Notify mapping, inspection, evidence,

00:00:58.654 --> 00:01:03.434
or analytics owners when location or time confidence is reduced so questionable

00:01:03.434 --> 00:01:07.019
data does not silently become an authoritative business record.

00:01:07.019 --> 00:01:09.409
Crew confusion can amplify the hazard.

00:01:09.409 --> 00:01:13.790
One person may trust the screen while another trusts visual cues.

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:20.164
Predefined communication and authority help the pilot prioritize aircraft control instead of debating attribution in flight.

00:01:20.164 --> 00:01:24.147
Safety priorities are people, other aircraft, property, and controlled recovery.

00:01:24.147 --> 00:01:26.537
Mission data and equipment come afterward.

00:01:26.537 --> 00:01:30.122
Reduce dependence on automation, preserve orientation and battery margin,

00:01:30.122 --> 00:01:35.300
select a safe landing, and document the anomaly once immediate risk is controlled.
